<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bahia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bahia is a state in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>, which was the colonial capital of Brazil until 1763. It was a major destination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, receiving many enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba people</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> collapsed in the nineteenth century. Yoruba religion and culture strongly shaped Afro-Brazilian traditions there, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bahia.jpg" alt="Bahia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diocleciano Ramos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandeira_da_Bahia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Bahia</b> sugar districts it was often cheaper to work people to death over a few years and replace them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Oyo-controlled trade networks are estimated to have contributed several hundred thousand captives to the Atlantic trade, and the wars that followed the empire’s collapse sent still larger numbers of Yoruba-speaking captives to the Americas in the early nineteenth century, above all to Brazil, particularly <b>Bahia</b>, and to Cuba.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hundreds of thousands of captives were sold into the Atlantic slave trade, many of them to Cuba and to <b>Bahia</b> in Brazil, where Yoruba-speaking people were called Lucumí and Nagô.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>On 7 November 1837 rebels led by the doctor Francisco Sabino Vieira took the city and proclaimed the <b>Bahia</b> Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/bahia-republic">Bahia Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The Recôncavo around the bay was filled with sugar plantations and tobacco farms, and Salvador was one of the main ports of the Atlantic slave trade, giving <b>Bahia</b> a strong African culture.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-bahia">Captaincy of Bahia</a></li><li><blockquote>The crown bought the captaincy from its proprietors in 1718, and it was placed under <b>Bahia</b> before regaining its own governor in 1810.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-espirito-santo">Captaincy of Espírito Santo</a></li></ul></section>
